\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces From primitive determinant divisors to prescribed prime-order periods. Flatters supplies primitive rational primes for positive norm-one quadratic units. Lemma\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {lem:primitive-kernel} converts a primitive divisor to exact finite-field and torus period. For negative trace, the conversion through \(B=-M\) is separate and uses primitive indices \(2n\), \(n\), and \(n/2\) in the three parity branches. The certified tail \(n>12\) is proof-derived. No tail period was computed.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:primitive-bridge}{{1}{4}{From primitive determinant divisors to prescribed prime-order periods. Flatters supplies primitive rational primes for positive norm-one quadratic units. Lemma~\ref {lem:primitive-kernel} converts a primitive divisor to exact finite-field and torus period. For negative trace, the conversion through \(B=-M\) is separate and uses primitive indices \(2n\), \(n\), and \(n/2\) in the three parity branches. The certified tail \(n>12\) is proof-derived. No tail period was computed}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{rem:half-index}{{3.4}{4}{Why the half-index branch is necessary}{theorem.3.4}{}}
\citation{Flatters2009Primitive}
